WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.141 --> 00:00:03.390
FELIPE HOFFA: Hello, everyone.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:04.280
I'm Felipe Hoffa.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.290
I'm a Google Cloud Platform
Developer Advocate.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.680
And today I'm at
the GitHub offices

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.260
to learn about how they
are using the BigQuery.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.870
You might already know
that BigQuery's great

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.970
when you went to analyze
terabytes of data in seconds.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.390
And that there are
a lot of open data

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:22.670
sets around that you can
query whenever you want.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.570
But BigQuery is not
only good for open data,

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.850
a lot of corporations,
enterprises,

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:31.000
companies are using
BigQuery to understand

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.690
their own data, their users,
their own metrics, et cetera.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:38.380
And today at GitHub I'm
going to look at both worlds.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.260
Because we have a lot
of, at least three open

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.070
datasets about
GitHub that they're

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.180
using to understand
their own company.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:51.580
So today, Alyson La, from the
data science team at GitHub

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:52.850
will join me.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.730
We will explore how they are
using BigQuery and other tools

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:58.720
and open data to understand
their organization.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.375
So Alyson, thank you so
much for inviting me.

00:01:02.375 --> 00:01:04.000
ALYSON LA: Yeah,
thanks for being here.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.760
