FELIPE HOFFA: How do with
these reports with Tableau?

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:37.690
ALYSON LA: So I create it
using the Tableau workbook,

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:39.100
which I have on my desktop.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:44.530
And I can do a direct
connect to Google BigQuery.

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:47.440
I just have to click
Google BigQuery

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.270
and enter my credentials.

00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:52.604
FELIPE HOFFA: Let's
keep your password safe.

00:11:55.768 --> 00:11:59.380
ALYSON LA: And sign in,
and then allow access.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:02.320
And then so once I've
connected my Tableau workbook

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:09.170
with BigQuery, I will
be able to see that it's

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:10.520
connected the server here.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:13.400
And I can select a
project here or the public

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:14.930
dataset we can at.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:19.129
But to access the GitHub Archive
I just type in GitHub Archive.

00:12:19.129 --> 00:12:20.670
FELIPE HOFFA: The
name of the project

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:21.753
that contains the dataset.

00:12:21.753 --> 00:12:22.820
ALYSON LA: Yeah.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:25.950
And then scroll down and look at
the dataset that I want to use.

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.860
So I want to use the month.

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:31.441
And I can scroll down to get
the most recent complete month,

00:12:31.441 --> 00:12:32.690
which would be September here.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.540
And I can drag that
over and update.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:40.120
And this is like doing, you
know, select star limit 10,

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:43.460
where I can see the
output of the table.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:49.350
And just kind of overview
what's in the table contents.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:51.710
And then from here, I
can go to the worksheet

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:54.740
and start dragging and dropping
to do some exploratory analysis

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:55.690
using Tableau.

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:57.230
FELIPE HOFFA: Si Tableau
understands all of the columns

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:58.304
you have here and--

00:12:58.304 --> 00:12:58.970
ALYSON LA: Yeah.

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:02.810
It already knows the dates
should be in a date format,

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:05.991
booleans are true,
false, numbers, strings.

00:13:05.991 --> 00:13:07.490
FELIPE HOFFA: So
for example, if you

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:10.240
want to know the
most frequent event.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:11.420
ALYSON LA: Yeah.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:15.530
So I could just select
type and number of records

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:19.030
and do show me bar chart.

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:22.700
And just to get an overview, I
can see, if I filter this down,

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.980
that push events are
the most common events

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:27.890
for this month, followed
by create events, watch

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:29.600
events and issue comments.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:32.270
FELIPE HOFFA: And if you want to
filter these events by any way.

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:33.980
ALYSON LA: Yeah, so you
can slice and dice it

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:35.104
a couple of different ways.

00:13:35.104 --> 00:13:38.930
You can drag the org
log in here to Filter.

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:42.530
And from there it will
allow you to choose,

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:46.130
say I can choose
Google as the name

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:48.372
of the organization on GitHub.

00:13:48.372 --> 00:13:49.830
FELIPE HOFFA: So
those are projects

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:53.490
hosted under the
Google organization.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:54.770
GitHub organization.

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:55.900
ALYSON LA: That's correct.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:57.200
FELIPE HOFFA: Now we
can see how people are

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:58.529
interacting with this project.

00:13:58.529 --> 00:13:59.570
ALYSON LA: Yeah, exactly.

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:02.900
So these are the different
events triggered,

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:07.954
filtered specifically on the
log-in for Google organization.

00:14:07.954 --> 00:14:09.620
You can see it's a
different shape here.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:12.650
Before it was mostly push
events and the most common event

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:15.750
type for the Google
organization was a watch event,

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.210
which is considered starring.

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:20.910
FELIPE HOFFA: How
many stars did we get?

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:25.133
ALYSON LA: 15,000.

00:14:25.133 --> 00:14:25.924
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:14:25.924 --> 00:14:27.770
And here you can
keep drilling in.

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.600
You can look at the
most popular projects,

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:30.544
what people are doing.

00:14:30.544 --> 00:14:31.210
ALYSON LA: Yeah.

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:33.590
FELIPE HOFFA: But you are
using not only Tableau here.

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:34.589
ALYSON LA: That's right.

00:14:34.589 --> 00:14:37.190
So recently I got
started using Looker,

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:41.720
which is also an analysis
tool similar to Tableau.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:43.190
But the nice thing
about Looker is

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:46.250
it allows you to do a data
connection to BigQuery.

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:48.650
But then build a
model layer, where

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:51.015
it allows you to
basically do pre-joins,

00:14:51.015 --> 00:14:51.890
if you want to do it.

00:14:51.890 --> 00:14:54.667
You can do the JSON extract
directly in the model layer.

00:14:54.667 --> 00:14:56.750
And we love it because
it's all version-controlled

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:58.010
using can GitHub.

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:01.340
So taking a look, here
is an example dashboard

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:04.140
using the GitHub web
archive latest events.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.650
So again, using a
short date range.

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.900
This is filtered to
just the last two days.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:10.460
This is an example
of the dashboard

00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:12.710
that you can use
to see the number

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:16.360
of active users, the number
of active organizations,

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:17.480
repositories.

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:21.590
You know, the event types,
developers by commit counts.

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:24.460
And again, most popular
languages here by pull request

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:25.197
account.

00:15:25.197 --> 00:15:27.530
And then you can filter it
to any organization you want.

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:31.490
So say, we can look at Angular
here, and see on the dashboard

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:33.860
that in this time
period of two days

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:37.230
there were 530 active users.

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:40.550
Anyone that interacted with
any repositories on Angular

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:43.970
organization, 65
active repositories.

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:46.370
And then you can see that
the most popular languages

00:15:46.370 --> 00:15:48.470
by pull request are a
little bit different.

00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:51.684
JavaScript, TypeScript,
HTML and Java.

00:15:51.684 --> 00:15:53.850
FELIPE HOFFA: And the
interesting thing about Looker

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:59.150
is it adds a layer where Looker
understands what the underlying

00:15:59.150 --> 00:15:59.840
data is.

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:02.307
ALYSON LA: Yeah, I can
show you that model layer.

00:16:02.307 --> 00:16:04.640
FELIPE HOFFA: So it's a model
that someone has to write?

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:05.180
ALYSON LA: That's right.

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:07.388
FELIPE HOFFA: But only one
person at the organization

00:16:07.388 --> 00:16:09.110
needs to understand.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:11.180
ALYSON LA: I think
helps for other people

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:16.100
to understand the model
and how it's built.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:19.570
But not everyone needs to know
how to write the model layer.

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:21.579
FELIPE HOFFA: So this
is GitHub's model.

00:16:21.579 --> 00:16:23.120
ALYSON LA: Yeah, so
this is the model

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.070
for all of the public events.

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:27.140
And Looker's really
neat because it

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:29.750
has this ID where
it tells you what

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:30.900
you have not committed yet.

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:33.200
So these green lines are things
that I still need to push up.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:34.325
FELIPE HOFFA: Using GitHub?

00:16:34.325 --> 00:16:36.482
ALYSON LA: Using GitHub, yeah.

00:16:36.482 --> 00:16:37.940
And I can just
press commit changes

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:40.130
and it will push it
up to the hosted Git

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:41.900
Repository on GitHub.

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:45.890
But for example, this
pull request action here,

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:48.200
I can do this JSON extract here.

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:50.630
And then I can
filter on the action.

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:52.910
So when I go to do the
analysis, I can just

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:55.880
pick action equals
opened or closed

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:58.940
and it allows me to
pick out the pre-filter

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:01.010
and do the JSON extract here.

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:03.300
FELIPE HOFFA: So anyone
that's working with Looker

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:05.599
doesn't need to understand
that if some data is

00:17:05.599 --> 00:17:06.790
[INAUDIBLE] JSON.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:09.655
The model takes care of
extracting that away.

00:17:09.655 --> 00:17:10.280
ALYSON LA: Yeah

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:11.160
FELIPE HOFFA:
That's really cool.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:12.680
What a lot of
people ask me is how

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:16.390
to translate these interesting
metrics into actionable advice.

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:21.486
Like what kind of things can you
do that translate into action?

00:17:21.486 --> 00:17:23.569
ALYSON LA: Yeah, so something
we're thinking about

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:28.369
internally is open source KPIs,
or Key Performance Indicators

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:32.540
using the BigQuery datasets.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:36.290
So things like open source
monthly active users

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:40.567
or monthly active repositories,
and tracking that over time.

00:17:40.567 --> 00:17:42.650
FELIPE HOFFA: And we can
look at people by region,

00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:44.480
how they are
interacting with GitHub.

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:48.590
ALYSON LA: Yeah, so another
way to measure user engagement

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:52.550
is looking at monthly active
users and daily active users,

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:56.900
and seeing the ratio by region
to see where some users are

00:17:56.900 --> 00:17:57.980
less engaged than others.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:18:01.100
And just really by region
how different regions

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:02.330
are using GitHub differently.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:04.860
FELIPE HOFFA: So this is
really, really awesome.

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:06.530
I love how anyone
at home can start

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:08.950
